  "graph": {
    "_note": "Visual parameters for the graph. Here rather than in the code so they can be tuned without a rebuild, and so a fork can look different without editing components. Not all of these are exposed in the interface, and they do not need to be \u2014 being reachable is the point.",
    "card": {
      "width": 180,
      "height": 140,
      "hoverWidth": 200,
      "hoverHeight": 160,
      "pinnedWidth": 230,
      "pinnedHeight": 190,
      "minWidth": 110,
      "minHeight": 80,
      "maxWidth": 620,
      "maxHeight": 520,
      "cornerRadius": 10,
      "glowPadding": 16,
      "labelMinFontSize": 14,
      "labelMaxFontSize": 36,
      "compactHeight": 260,
      "imageMarkSize": 22
    },
    "tag": {
      "fontSize": 22,
      "padding": 11,
      "maxWidth": 150,
      "maxLines": 3,
      "cornerRadius": 9,
      "opacity": 0.7
    },
    "timeAxis": {
      "dock": "left",
      "_dock_note": "left | right | top | bottom. The axis is a ruler pinned to the edge of the viewport, not an object in the canvas: panning and zooming the corpus moves the corpus past it, the way a ruler stays put while the paper slides under it. A docked edge also decides the direction \u2014 a side rail runs earliest at the top, a top or bottom rail earliest at the left.",
      "inset": 54,
      "endPadding": 70,
      "connectorOpacity": 0.45,
      "connectorWidth": 1.6,
      "spineOpacity": 0.55,
      "spineWidth": 3,
      "tickFontSize": 13
    },
    "simulation": {
      "linkDistance": 160,
      "chargeStrength": -500,
      "collidePadding": 10,
      "velocityDecay": 0.7,
      "alphaDecay": 0.028
    }
  }
